WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.471
How does FundsXML evolve, and how can you take part?

2
00:00:03.521 --> 00:00:14.624
In this film you see who decides about the standard, which working groups there are, how to join them, how a change request becomes part of a new version, and what makes a good change request.

3
00:00:15.874 --> 00:00:17.704
Why should you take part at all?

4
00:00:17.754 --> 00:00:20.701
Because the standard follows the people who use it.

5
00:00:20.751 --> 00:00:25.813
Regulation changes every year, new templates appear, and markets bring new instruments.

6
00:00:25.863 --> 00:00:29.044
Every change to FundsXML affects many partners at once.

7
00:00:29.094 --> 00:00:37.388
Whoever takes part brings in their own requirements early, hears about changes before they arrive, and can plan their own systems accordingly.

8
00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:41.060
FundsXML belongs to no single company.

9
00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:46.178
It is run by FundsXML dot org, a not-for-profit organisation of the fund industry.

10
00:00:46.228 --> 00:00:48.363
Its rules are set out in a charter.

11
00:00:48.413 --> 00:00:51.546
At the top stands the FundsXML Standards Committee.

12
00:00:51.596 --> 00:00:59.973
A secretariat organises the work, working groups do the work on the standard, and members and the wider community bring in their needs.

13
00:01:01.323 --> 00:01:04.647
The Standards Committee, or F S C, is the governing body.

14
00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:10.803
Its members are fund companies, service providers and fund associations from several European countries.

15
00:01:10.853 --> 00:01:12.638
It meets at least twice a year.

16
00:01:12.688 --> 00:01:22.641
It decides on new members, it sets up the working groups and names their chairs, and it decides when the results of a working group become a new version of the standard.

17
00:01:23.991 --> 00:01:26.907
The work itself happens in three working groups.

18
00:01:26.957 --> 00:01:35.125
Content and Technique is the technical heart: it maintains the schema, designs new data structures and keeps the standard consistent.

19
00:01:35.175 --> 00:01:40.253
Promotion makes FundsXML known across Europe and talks to new members and partners.

20
00:01:40.303 --> 00:01:48.409
And Documentation creates the guides, examples and references that help users implement the standard, including films like this one.

21
00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:51.245
There are three ways in.

22
00:01:51.295 --> 00:01:53.420
First, become an associate member.

23
00:01:53.470 --> 00:02:00.906
It is free, any company or person can apply on the website, and members receive the news and invitations of FundsXML.

24
00:02:00.956 --> 00:02:10.503
Second, join a working group: on the About page of fundsxml dot org, a short form asks for your name, your e-mail and the group you are interested in.

25
00:02:10.553 --> 00:02:15.942
Third, contribute on GitHub, where the schema, the examples and the tools are public.

26
00:02:17.292 --> 00:02:22.432
Full membership is meant for fund companies, asset managers and national fund associations.

27
00:02:22.482 --> 00:02:26.310
The Standards Committee decides on it, and full members have a vote.

28
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.986
More than a hundred and ten companies support FundsXML as associate members today.

29
00:02:31.036 --> 00:02:38.970
Both kinds of members are invited to take part in the working groups, and at least once a year there is a meeting for the associate members.

30
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.864
On GitHub, the organisation fundsxml publishes the schema, the example files, and also these videos.

31
00:02:46.914 --> 00:02:58.734
In the schema repository you can follow every change: issues describe problems and wishes, pull requests contain proposed changes, and the changelog lists what each version brings.

32
00:02:58.784 --> 00:03:02.163
Anyone can read along, and anyone can open an issue.

33
00:03:03.513 --> 00:03:07.519
The most direct way to shape the standard is a change request.

34
00:03:07.569 --> 00:03:12.099
Anyone can submit one: a member, a software provider or a single user.

35
00:03:12.149 --> 00:03:20.538
A change request may be a new data field, a new code value, a correction, or a whole new area, such as a new regulatory template.

36
00:03:21.888 --> 00:03:23.943
This is the path of a change request.

37
00:03:23.993 --> 00:03:30.541
It is submitted in writing to the working group or to a member of the Standards Committee, or as an issue on GitHub.

38
00:03:30.591 --> 00:03:34.125
The working group decides whether it goes into a working draft.

39
00:03:34.175 --> 00:03:35.376
The draft is reviewed.

40
00:03:35.426 --> 00:03:41.463
And when it is approved, it becomes part of the next version of the schema, with an entry in the changelog.

41
00:03:42.813 --> 00:03:46.071
How long the review takes depends on the size of the change.

42
00:03:46.121 --> 00:03:55.665
Small changes, such as new optional fields or corrections that keep existing files valid, are decided by the working group, and the committee has five working days to object.

43
00:03:55.715 --> 00:04:03.715
Large changes, which alter the structure or break compatibility, need a vote of the committee and a public comment period of at least twenty days.

44
00:04:05.065 --> 00:04:05.690
An example.

45
00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:13.852
Today a document in FundsXML carries its size, but no checksum, so a receiver cannot be sure that an embedded file arrived unchanged.

46
00:04:13.902 --> 00:04:20.664
A change request could propose an optional checksum element with the algorithm, for example S H A two five six.

47
00:04:20.714 --> 00:04:25.942
Since it is optional and keeps existing files valid, it would count as a small change.

48
00:04:27.292 --> 00:04:30.463
Here is how such a request could look as an issue.

49
00:04:30.513 --> 00:04:31.364
A short title.

50
00:04:31.414 --> 00:04:36.646
The use case: receivers of embedded key information documents want to verify them.

51
00:04:36.696 --> 00:04:43.087
The proposal: an element Checksum after the size, with the value and an attribute for the algorithm.

52
00:04:43.137 --> 00:04:44.375
A small XML example.

53
00:04:44.425 --> 00:04:49.013
And the note that the element is optional, so existing files stay valid.

54
00:04:50.363 --> 00:04:52.389
What makes a good change request?

55
00:04:52.439 --> 00:04:55.911
Describe the business case: who needs the data, and why.

56
00:04:55.961 --> 00:05:00.126
Define the data point exactly: its meaning, its type and its codes.

57
00:05:00.176 --> 00:05:01.824
Add a short example in XML.

58
00:05:01.874 --> 00:05:04.214
Say whether existing files stay valid.

59
00:05:04.264 --> 00:05:06.667
And name the partners who would use it.

60
00:05:06.717 --> 00:05:10.064
A clear request saves the working group a lot of time.

61
00:05:11.414 --> 00:05:18.111
That was how to take part in FundsXML: the committee, the working groups, membership and change requests.

62
00:05:18.161 --> 00:05:22.738
You find the contact form on fundsxml dot org, and the schema on GitHub.

63
00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:26.722
The next series shows FundsXML in a real file, for developers.

64
00:05:26.772 --> 00:05:28.007
Thanks for watching.
